Macedonians Vote in Referendum to Relaunch Your Country's Name Change
Macedonian citizens voted in a referendum on Saturday if they wanted to change their country's "Republika of North Macedonia". It is a move that could resolve a dispute with Greece that has been going on for several decades and for which Greece has blocked the opening negotiations for accession to the European Union and NATO.
In Greece, there's a province called Macedonia and Greece argues that the name of its northern Neighbour represents a claim of its territory and has vested a vet for the country's entry into NATO and the EU.
In June, the two governments agreed on a proposed new name, but nationalist opponents argue that the change would undermine the ethnic identity of Macedonia's majority Slavic population.
President Gjorge Ivanov has stated that he will not vote in the referendum, and the campaign to boycott the vote has cast doubt on whether the turnout will meet the minimum 50 per cent needed in order for the referendum to be valid.
The question in the voting ballot said: "Are you in favour of membership in NATO and the EU with the acceptance of a deal with Greece?"
Consents of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that it is a price worth paying in order to enable Macedonia, one of the countries created after the break-wregation of Yugoslavia, to join bodies such as the EU and NATO.
"Today I came to vote for the future of the country, for the youth of Macedonia so that we can live freely under the auspices of the European Union because it means safer lives for all of us," Olivera Georgijevska, 79, said in Skopje.
"While the result of the ballot is not legally binding, it has been said that members of parliament will respect it to be considered decisive.
The name change requires a two-three-magnificent majority in parliament.
The State Electoral Commission said no irregularities had been reported until 1 p.m.
But the turnout was only 16 per cent, compared with 34 per cent during the last mid-laden midterm elections in 2016, when 66 per cent of registered voters voted.
"I have come to vote because of my children, it's our place in Europe," Gjose Tanevski, a 62-year-old voter from Skopje's capital, said.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and son Dusko in Strumica, took a referendum to change the name of the country on September 30, 2018, which would pave the way for joining the NATO alliance and the European Union.
54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkov in front of parliament in Skopulje set up a small stage and received chairs in front of tents set up by those boycotting the referendum.
“We are in favor of NATO and the EU, but we want to join the eclipsed heads, not as servants,” Kavadarkov said.
“We are a poor country, but we have dignity.
“Unfortunately they don’t want to receive us under the name of Macedonia, we can turn to others like China and Russia and become part of Euro-Asian integration.”
Prime Minister Zaev has said that NATO membership will bring much-neied investment to Macedonia, a country where the unemployment rate exceeds 20 per cent.
"I believe that the vast majority will support the referendum, since more than 80 percent of our citizens are supported by the EU and NATO," Zaev said.
Said a "positive" result would represent a "confirmation of our future."
A survey released last Monday by the Macedonian Institute of Political Research shows that between 30 and 43 per cent of voters will join the remanument, which is below the necessary turnout.
Another poll, carried out by Macedonia's Telma TV, showed 57 per cent of respondents intend to vote on Sunday.
Of that number, 70 per cent said they would support the referendum.
In order for the referendum to be successful, the turnout must be 50 per cent plus one vote.
Failed a referendum would be the first serious blow to a pro-Western-composuscity policy since it took office in May last year.
