"Sea is ours": Bolivia, which has no access to the sea, is hoping that the court will allocate the exit to the Silent Ocean
Sailors are patrolled by a naval centre full of cranes in La Paz.
An ocean-coloured flag was hinted on public buildings.
Naval bases from Lake Titincica to the Amazon are motled: “We have the right to the sea.
"It's our duty to get him back."
Throughout Bolivia, which doesn't have access to the sea, the remembrance of a lost sea coast in the favor of Chile during conflict due to resources in the 19th-century is still alive - just like the longing for rejoises by the Pacific.
While Bolivia is awaiting a ruling by the International Court of Justice on October 1, after a five-year trial, and hopes are perhaps at its highest level in decades.
"Bolivia has a momentum, a spirit of unity and peace and, of course, is optimistically awaiting the outcome," said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Plenty of Bolivians across the country will watch the International Court of Justice's ruling on large screens and hope that a court in The Hague will decide in favor of Bolivia's view that Chile, after decades of demanding negotiations, is obliged to negotiate the provision of a sovereign exit to the Sea of Bolivia.
Evo Morales, a Bolivian charismatic autochthonous president who faces a controversial battle to be re-elected next year, can also benefit from Monday's verdict.
"We are within the return of the Pacific Ocean," he pledged at the end of August.
But some analysts don't believe the court is unlikely to rule in favor of Bolivia and that little would change if it did.
The U.N. governing body based in the Netherlands doesn't authorise Chilean territory and has stated that it will not determine the outcome of possible negotiations.
The fact that the judgment of the International Court of Justice comes just six months after the final hearings means that the case "has not been complex", said Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert on international law.
Instead of embarrassing the interests of Bolivia, the last four years may have devastated.
"The trajectory of access to the sea has been appropriated by the current Bolivian leadership," Ms. Zárate said.
Morale's belligerent rhetoric cultivated every good will of Chile, he believes.
Bolivia and Chile will continue the dialogue at some point, but after this it will be extremely difficult to negotiate.
These two countries have not exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Ex-president Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, a Bolivian representative in The Hague, does not agree with the view that the court made the decision uncharacteristically quickly.
On Monday, Bolivia will bring an "surprisible opportunity to start a new period in relations with Chile" and the opportunity to "interrule 139 years of disagreement with mutual benefit," he said.
Calzadilla also said Morales - still one of the most beloved presidents in Latin America - used the maritime issue as a political crutsp.
"Bolivia will never renounce the right to access the Pacific Ocean," he added.
"The verdict is an opportunity for us to realize that we need to overcome the past."
