John Edward: the language skills that the citizens of the world need
Scottish independent schools have a tradition of academia, which continued in 2018 with another package of outstanding exam results, and additional weight is given by individual and joint achievements in sports, arts, music and other activities in the community.
With more than 30,000 students across Scotland, this school represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), they are striving to provide the best level of service to their students and their parents.
Indecent schools aim to prepare students for further education and higher education, a career they want and make them citizens of the world.
As an education sector that can design and implement this school curricula, we see that modern languages continue to be a favorite and preferred subject of choice in schools.
Nelson Mandela stated: "If you turn to a man in a language he understands, it goes into his head.
“Educating himself in his mother’s tongue, it goes into his heart.”
This statement is a stark reminder that we cannot only be used in English if we want to build trust and relationships with people from other countries.
The most recent results of the final exams this year show that the highest grades in the languages were achieved by the participants of independent schools.
A total of 68% of students who learned foreign languages received excellent grades.
Data collected in 74 SCIS member schools show that 72% of pupils received an excellent tabernatic grade, while 72% of students learning German, 69% of pupils learning French and 63% of Spanish students also received the highest possible grade.
This shows that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as a key skill that children and young people will certainly need in the future.
Today, languages as elective subjects are considered as important as STEM subjects (scientuality, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the curricula of independent schools, as well as in other places.
A survey carried out by the U.K (WF&Ma7 % of Employergies in 2014 has found that 17% of employers are unable to fill jobs due to a lack of language skills.
Therefore, language skills are increasingly becoming an imperative for the preparation of young people for their careers.
Since more and more jobs are looking for knowledge of languages, these skills are crucial in the globalized world.
Regardless of the choice of a career, people who have learned to speak a foreign language in the future will feel the benefits of such a long-term skill.
A multi-speaking person can directly communicate with strangers, and this prioritizes them in relation to the competition.
According to a YouGov survey in which more than 4000 adults participated in 2013, 75% of respondents in the United Kingdom don't know a foreign language well enough to be able to communicate on it, and French is the only language spoken by a double-digit percentage of respondents, namely 15 %.
Therefore, investing in language learning is very important for today's children.
If children learn multiple languages, in particular the languages of the developing economy, there will be more opportunities to find a good job.
In Scotia, every school offers a variety of languages.
Some schools will include classic, modern languages in its program, while others will opt for languages that are thought to be most important in the United Kingdom in 2020, for example Mandarin or Japanese.
Regardless of the interests of your child, in independent schools it will always have a diverse choice, as well as teaching staff who are professional in the area.
Scottish-essential schools provide children with an environment in which, by learning, they will refine the skills needed to succeed, no matter what the future saves us.
It cannot be ignored the fact that in today's global business knowledge of language is very important for the future of every country so it must also be reflected in education.
Thus, the knowledge of modern languages should indeed be viewed as "international communication skills".
Appropriate schools will continue to offer language selection, diversity and excellence to young Scots.
Il fout bien le faire.
John Edward Director of the Scottish Independent School Council
