North Korea says "there's no chance" of unilateral disarmament without trust
North Korea's foreign minister told the U.S. on Saturday that further sanctions increase distrust in the United States and there's no way North Korea would unilaterally disarmament and give up its nuclear weapons in such circumstance.
Ri Yong Ho told the U.N. General Assembly that North Korea took "significant goodwill measures" last year, for example halting nuclear and missile tests, dismantling a nuclear testing station and made a decision to halt the development of nuclear technology and weapons.
"However, we didn't get a satisfying reaction from the U.S.," he added.
“If we have no confidence in the U.S. don’t, we don’t have faith in our national security. Under such circumvention, there’s no prospect of unilaterally dismantling.”
"Although Ri reiterated North-Serbie-densified complaints about Washington's endorsement of a "complective" approach of denuclearization in which North Korea would gradually repeal the sanction, its statement is significant because it has not rejected unilateral denuclearization as Pyongyang did in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at the first meeting in the history of the incumbent U.S. president and North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, when Kim made a promise to work to "denuclearize the Korean Peninsula," while Trump assured North Korea's security.
North Korea wants to formally end the Korean War that lasted from 1950 to 1953, but the U.S. conditioned Pyongyang to renounce nuclear weapons beforehand.
Washington has also ignored calls to reduce strict international sanctions on North Korea.
"The U.S. insists that denuclearization be carried out beforehand and increasing pressure by means of sanitisers in order to forcibly achieved its goal, and even opposes "declaration of an end to war," Ri added.
“Perceistance that sanctions can bring us to their knees is just a misconception of people who don't know anything about us.
The main problem is that the continuation of sanction increases milieu."
Ri didn't mention whether there are plans for a second meeting between Kim and Trump that the U.S. president was talking about earlier this week at the U.N.
But the minister pointed out that Kim and the leader of South Korea Moon Jae-in held as many as three meetings in the last five months and added: "If denuclearization had been demanded by South Korea, not the U.S., the denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula would not have reached such a stalemate."
Nonetheless, the tone of Minister Rio's speech differed greatly from last year when he spoke before the U.N.'s General Assembly and stated that targeting the continental U.S. missiles was unavoidable after evil President Trump called Kim a "human rocket" on a suicidal mission.
Trump, who threatened North Korea "complete demise" last year at the United Nations, praised Kim for his courage to disarmament, but added that there was a lot of work awaiting them and that sanctions must remain in place until North Korea exerts denuclearization.
On Wednesday, Trump said there is no timeline for denuclearization and added: "It doesn't matter whether it's going to last for two years, three years or five months."
China and Russia have expressed a tribute that the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for measures taken.
But U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo to the U.N. Security Council said on Thursday: "Improfessibility of the Sanctions of the Security Council must strictly continue until there is a complete, definitive and vet-verified denuclearization."
The Security Council unanimously imposed sanctions on North Korea in 2006 in an attempt to halt funding for nuclear and ballistic missile programs in Pyongyang.
Pompeo met with Rio at the U.N.'s general meeting and then stated that he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for a second meeting.
Pompeo visited North Korea three times this year, but his last visit did not go well.
Pyongyang left Pyongyang in July that progress had been made, but North Korea called it out after a few o'clock because of "gangster demands."
North-Centrus has agreed a meeting with the Moon this month to dismantle a missile site and a nuclear-sealing complex if the U.S. takes "appropriate measures."
He added that Kim had revealed that these “approprietary measures” were related to the security guarantees that Trump had promised in Singapore and certain steps towards stabilising relations with Washington.
Students at Harvard take a course to learn how to get a good rest
A new course at Harvard University has made it possible for all students to get a good sleep in trying to combat an increasingly widespread culture of learning alongside caffeine and sleepless nights.
The academician found that students at the world's best universities often have no idea about the basics of self-care.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleeping science at Harvard School of Medicine and a specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, devised a course he considers to be the first of its kind in the U.S.
He came up with the idea of the course after giving a speech about the effect of sleep deprivation on learning.
At the end of the lecture, I was approached by a girl and said: “Why didn’t I find this out right now, at the last year of college?”
'She said she didn't tell her how important the dream was, which surprised me," he told The Telegraph.
For the first time this year's course, explains to students that good sleep habits help in academic and sporting success and generally improve health.
Psychiatric Physicism at Harvard Medical School and Paul Barreira Healthcare Healthcare Service executive says that the University has decided to introduce a course after a serious lack of sleep has been spotted in students during the week.
A one-hour course consists of a series of interactive tasks.
One of these quests involves a picture of a dormitory on which students have to lew coffee cups, curtains, sneakers and books to find out the effects of caffeine and light, how the lack of sleep affects success and how important sleep routine is.
During the lecture, students also find out that long-term lack of sleep can increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression and cancer.
Subsequently, a campus map with interactive icons is displayed that encourages students to reflect on their daily routine.
“We don’t know that student behavior will not change immediately.
But we think they have the right to know, just as you have the right to know how cigarettes affect your health," Professor Czeisler added.
The culture of pride on all-night learning before the exam still exists, says the professor and adds that modern technology and the increasing pressure on students prove that lack of sleep is a growing problem.
The professor says that a secret weapon of students against stress, exhaustion and anguish should be a good and high-quality sleep. Sleep also helps to combat weight gain because the lack of sleep affects the brain, throws it into a state of starvation and causes constant faculties.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old chemistry and biology student from California, helped Professor Czeisler devise a course after attending his lectures at Harvard's first year.
He says that the course opened his eyes and imprisoned him at extending the course to the entire campus.
As the next step, he plans to ask all postgraduates to complete a similar study course prior to joining a competitor.
Professor Czeisler advises students to turn on the alarm during sleep, as well as during waking up. They need to be aware of the harmful effects of "blue light" emitted by displays of electronic devices, as well as LED lights that can disrupt the circadian rhythm and cause sleep problems.
