What is the Global Citizen Festival and has it achieved anything in terms of poverty reduction?
This sabbath, New York will host the Global Citizen Festival, an annual music event with an ultra-impressive list of stars and an equally immersive mission – ending poverty in the world.
For the seventh year in a row Global Citizen Festival will bring together tens of thousands of people in the Central Park to enjoy not only the performances of stars, such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness of the real goal of the event, ending extreme poverty by 2030.
Global Citizen Festival, which began in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an international advocacy groups hoping to end poverty by increasing the number of people who are actively battling it.
In order to obtain a free ticket for an event (unless they were willing to pay for a VIP ticket), concert visitors had to carry out a series of assignments or "workings" such as volunteering, e-mail to the world leader, phone calls or any other meaningful ways to raise awareness of their goal of ending poverty.
But how successful does Global Citizen really take into account that it has 12 years left to achieve the goal?
Whether the idea of rewarding people with a free-concert is a real way of persuading people to demand an invitation to act, or just another case of so-called “clinctivisms” – where people feel that by signing an online petition or by sending a tweet they really change something?
Since 2011, Global Citizen has claimed that it has recorded more than 19 million “actions” of its supporters who have strived to achieve a range of different goals.
He argues that these actions were an impetuary for world leaders to announce commitments and policies that will lead to more than $37 billion that will have an impact on the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
