Inventor of the World Wide Web plans to launch a new internet to download Google and Facebook
Berners-Lee, an inventor of the World Wide Web, is launching a new company that aims to become a computation to Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The most recent project of this technology legend, Inrupt, is a company that develops based on Berners-Lee's open platform Solid.
Solid allows users to choose a place of storage of their data and people who will be allowed access to certain information.
In an exquisi-in-command for Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that the intention behind Inruption was "world dominance."
"We have to do it now," he said, referring to a startup.
"It's a historic moment."
The application uses Solid's technology to enable people to create their own "personal network data storage" or FER.
It may contain contact lists, comresponsibilities lists, a calendar, a music library and other personal and professional tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available on one browser at the same time at the same time.
What's unambiguous when it comes to personal online data storage is that it's a decision on who is able to access what information fully users.
The company calls it "personal empowerment through data".
The Idea for Inrupt, according to John Bruce's chief executive, is for the company to provide resources, processes, and appropriate skills to help Solid be accessible to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IBM, several engineers who have signed a contract to work on the project, as well as a community of voluntary developers.
Beginning from this week, developers from around the world were able to create their own decentralized applications using tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said that he and his team do not talk to Facebook and Google about whether or not to impose a complete change in which all of their business models would be completely reversed overnight.
“We don’t ask for their permit.”
In a post at Medium published on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt's "mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem in order to protect the integrity and quality of a new network built on Solidarity."
In 1994. Berners-Lee transformed the internet when he founded a Conservation of the World Wide Web at the Massachusetts (MIT).
In recent months Berners-Lee has had an influential voice in the network's neutrality debate.
Even as it launches Inrupt, Berners-Lee will remain the founding and director of the Consortium of the World Wide Web, the World Wide Web Foundation.
"I am incredibly optimistic about the next era of the network," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: Celebration on the decoration of priests from the First World War Via Viktoria's Cross
Celebration in the honor of the only Priest of the Anglican Church that was decorated with Viktoria's cross as a fighter in the First World War was organized in his hometown 100 years later .
Lieutenant-madie Religious Bernard Vann won an award on September 29, 1918 in an attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
However, four days later he was killed by a sniper and he never found out that he had received Britain's biggest military tribute.
A memorial plaque was unveiled by two of his grandchildren at a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire on Saturday.
One of his grandchildren, Michael Vann, said it was "unbelievably symbolic" that the stone will be revealed exactly 100 years after his grandfather's comvro's feat for which he received this recognition.
According to a report by the Official Gazette of the London Gazette, on September 29, 1918, Lieutenant Colonel Vann led his battalion across the channel of St. Quentin "through very dense fog and under a strong firing coming from the direction of the battlefield and the machine gun."
He later rushed into the front rows and with "incredible courage" led the unit forward before he rushed to a Polish cannon without any help and mowed down three from a military detachment.
Lieutenant Colonel Vanna was assassinated by a German sniper on October 4, 1918 – a little more than a month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann, 72, said his grandfather's actions were "something I know I would never have been able to achieve, which arouses humility."
He and his brother Dr. James Vann also laid a wreath after the parade, which was hosted by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band Parade Orchestra.
Michael Vann said he "feel very honored to participate in the parade" and added that "couragement of a true hero is shown through the support given by a lot of people."
