Germany's new warship re-attached on hold
The German navy's rehearsal was supposed to be commissioned in 2014 to replace old warships from the Cold War, but it won't be ready until at least next year due to faulty systems and cost-rise, local media reported.
Putting into the traffic of ‘Rheinland-Pfalz, the leading ship of all-new Baden-Wuerttemberg class frigates, has now been delayed until the first half of 2019, the Die Zeit newspaper citing a military spokeswoman.
The ship was supposed to join the navy in 2014, but troubling problems after delivery struck the fate of an ambitious project.
Four Baden-Wuerttemberg-Baden-Wuerttemberg-class vessels, commissioned as far back in 2007, will be replaced by old Bremen class frigates.
As far as it is, it will have a powerful cannon, a series of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as some invisibility technologies, such as reduced radar, infrared and acutic signatures.
Other important features include a longer maintenance period  – it should be possible to allocate the latest frigates over a period of up to a two-year without returning to their home ports.
Continuous delays, however, mean that state-of-the-art warships – which are said to allow Germany to be able to show power abroad – by the time of entry into service, Die Zeit notes.
Unfortunate F125 frigate last year peaked on all front pages, when the German navy officially refused to put the ship into service and returned it to the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
It's the first time the navy has repatriated the ship after delivery.
There was little known about the reasons for the return, but the German media cited a number of key "software and hardware gaps" that would make the warship useless if it were sent on a combat mission.
Software gaps were particularly important because Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships will be operated by a crew of about 120 sailors – half less than on older Bremen class frigates.
It has also been shown that the ship is dramatically too severe which reduces its efficiency and limits the Navy's ability to add future upgrades.
"Rheinland-Pfalz" of 7000 tons is believed to be twice heavier than ships of a similar class used by the Germans in the Second World War.
In addition to malfunctional hardware, the price of the entire project – including crew training – it also becomes a problem.
It is said that the price reached a staggering €3.1 billion ($3.6bn) – compared to an initial €2.2bn.
Problems affecting the latest frigates are becoming particularly important in the light of recent warnings that Germany's naval force is shrinking.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, head of Germany's Defense Parliament's Committee, acknowledged that the navy "disappears ships capable of deploying."
The official said this problem increased over time, because the old ships were deteriorated, and there were no replacement vessels.
He expressed regression that none of Baden-Württemberg class frigates can join the Navy.
National Foundation eavesdropping on the secret life of a bat
The new research conducted on an estate in the Scottish Highlands aims to find out how the bats use the landscape in food hunting.
There's a hope that the results will clarify the behaviour of these unique flying mammals and help guide future conservation activities.
A study carried out by scientists at the Scottish (NTS National Foundation) will be followed by ordinary and swamp dwarf bats, as well as brown-mannered long-earned and river bats at Inverewe gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be placed in key places around the property to monitor bat activities throughout the season.
NTS staff and volunteers will also carry out mobile previews using handheld detectors.
An expert analysis of the sounds of all the recordings will determine the frequency of bat voting and what species does.
A habitat map and a report will then be created to create a detailed picture of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, NTS Nature Conservation Advisor, is hoped the results will reveal which habitat areas are most important for bats and how each species uses them.
This information will help determine the benefits of working on habitat management, such as the formation of meadows, and how to best maintain forest areas for bats and other related species.
Bat populations in Scotia and across the United Kingdom have declined significantly over the last century.
They are jeopardized by the construction and renovation works that affect shelters and habitat loss.
Victurbs and lighting can also pose a danger, as well as muchloches and certain chemical treatments of building materials, as well as pet cat attacks.
Bats aren’t really blind.
However, because of their hunting habits at night, their ears are more useful from the eyes when it comes to grabbing prey.
They use a sophisticated echolocation technique to find bugs and obstacles on the trajectory of their flight.
The NTS, which is responsible for caring for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 acres of land throughout the country, are taking bats very seriously.
There are ten trained specialists, who regularly carry out researches, shelter inspections and sometimes save.
The organization has even set up the first and only reserve for bats in Scotia at Threave in Dumfries and Galloway, home to eight out of ten bat species that inhabit Scotiska.
Warden, David Thompson, he's saying the property is the ideal area for them.
"Here in Treave, we've got a lot of space for bats," he said.
"We've got old buildings, a lot of old trees and all good habitats.
However, there's still a lot that we don't even know about bats, so the work we're doing here and on other facilities will help us better understand what they need for progress."
Emphasizes the importance of verifying the presence of bats before starting maintenance work inside the buildings, since the unintentional destruction of one litter can kill up to 400 females and young, thus probably destroying the entire local population.
Shrifters are protected and illegal to kill, torture or harass or destroy their shelters.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a representative for the Scottish Association for the Protection of Bats, encouraged the public to help.
“There’s still a lot to learn about our bats and we simply don’t know how their population is progressing.”
