BMW, for example, says it will close its Mini factory in Oxford for a month after Brexit.
The automaker's main concern relates to supply chain risks in the event of a no-death without reaching a deal.
Toyota's production line works according to the "just in time", where parts arrive every 37 minutes from suppliers in the UK and the EU for custom-made cars.
If the UK leaves the EU without an agreement on March 29, there could be a break at the border that the industry says could lead to delays and a lack of parts.
Toyota at its factory in Derbyshire would not be able to hold more supplies than those needed for a day, the company said, so production would be halted.
Mr. Clark said the "Chequers" plan offered by Theresa May for future relations with the EU was "precisely calibrated to avoid those border checks."
