According to New Conservative Plans, foreign buyers will be levied a higher levy when buying a real estate in the United Kingdom, and additional money will be used to help homeless
This step will neutralise the success of Corbyn's endeavor to deprive young voters
Increasing levies will be imposed on those who don't lend taxes in the United Kingdom.
Treasury Expects to Raise Up to £120 Million a Year to Help the Homeless
Theresa May will announce today that foreign buyers will be levied a higher levy when buying real estate in the United Kingdom – and additional money will be used to help the homeless.
This move will be understood as an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corbyn's bivalve streak efforts to demise young voters with promises to provide more favourable housing solutions and focus on high-wage ones.
Rate increases will be levied from individuals and non-tax-paying companies in the United Kingdom, and additional money will contribute to the Government's efforts to combat homelessness.
Additional levies  – which is added to the current fee, including an increase imposed two years ago on other homes and real estate purchased for the purpose of issuance – it can be up to three per cent.
The treasury expects that this move will collect £120 million a year.
An estimated 13 per cent of newly-constructed real estate in London is bought by residents who do not reside in the U.C., which raises prices and makes it difficult for buyers who buy their first property to get involved in the company of those who have their own home.
Many wealthy areas in the country – especially in the capital – become “ghost cities” due to a large number of foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the country.
The new policy came just a few weeks after Boris Johnson called for levies to help more young people reach their first home.
He accused major construction companies of maintaining high real estate prices by seizing but not exploiting the country, and urged Ms. May to abandon quotas on affordable real estate in order to repair "shame over the housing situation" in Britain.
