Donggala is a district that stretches along more than 300 km of coastline in the north-west island of Sulawesia.
The district, an administrative region lower than the province, had an estimated population of 299 200 in 2017.
Fishing and agriculture are the foundation of the Sulawesi province's economy, especially the coastal region of Donggal.
Nickel mines are also important in the province, but are mostly concentrated in Morowali, the opposing Sulawesi coast.
Palu and Donggala have been plagued by tsunami several times in the last 100 years, according to the Indonesian Disaster Mitigation Agency.
In 1938 tsunami killed more than 200 people and devastated hundreds of houses in Dongga.
Tsunami also hit western Donggal in 1996, killing nine people.
Indonesia is located on a Seismic Pacific Fire Circuit and is regularly plagued by earthquakes.
Here are some of the major earthquakes and tsunamis in recent years:
2004: A large earthquake on the west coast of the Aceh province in northern Sumatra on December 26 sparked a tsunami that struck 14 countries, killing 226,000 people along the Indian Ocean coast, more than halve in Aceho.
2005: A series of powerful earthquakes struck the west coast of Sumatra in late March and the beginning of April.
Hundreds of people were killed on Nias Island, off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: 6.8 deteriorated south of Java, Indonesia's most pono-neighbouring island, sparking a tsunami that slammed the southern coast, killing nearly 700 people.
2009: A 7.6-magnitude quarreout struck the town of Padang, West Sumatra province.
More than 1100 people were killed.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude quarries hit one of Mentawai islands, near Sumatra, provoking a tsunami of up to 10 meters that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: Shallow quake hit Pidie Jaya region in Aceh, causing destruction and panic as it reminded people of the devastration of a deadly earthquake and tsunami in 2004.
There hasn't been a tsunami this time, but more than 100 people have been killed a result of falling buildings.
2018: Huge quarrels hit the Indonesian tourist island of Lombok, killing more than 500 people, mostly on the north side of the island.
The quake devastated thousands of buildings and temporarily blockged thousands of tourists.
