Federal Prosecutors Seek Rare Death Penalty for Suspect for New York Terrorist Assault
Federal prosecutors in New York are demanding the death penalty for Sayfull Saipov, a suspect in a New York terrorist attack that killed eight people—an rare sentence unveiled in the state since 1953.
Saipov (30) reportedly used a Home Depot store rental truck to carry out an attack on a bicycle path along the West Side road in Donji Manhattan, where he mowed pedestrians and cyclists who were on his way in October
In order to justify the death penalty, the plaintiffs will have to prove that Saipov has “deliberately” killed eight victims and “deliberately” inflicted serious bodily injury, according to a notice of intent to seek the death penalty, which was filed in the Southern District of New York.
Both of these points lead to an eventual death penalty, in accordance with a judicial document.
A couple of weeks after the attack, a federal grand-flight Saipov slammed a 22-strong indictee that involved eight murder and extortion charges that federal plaintiffs typically use in organised crime cases, and allegations of violence and destruction of motor vehicles.
The assault required “significant planning and premeditation,” the plaintiffs said, blasphesing how Saipov performed it as ‘angular, ruthless and corrupt’.
"Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused injuries, damage and losses to families and friends of Dieg Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laura Decadt, Dareren Drake, Ariel Eryli, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandra Damiano Pagnucco," the statement said.
Five of the casualties were tourists from Argentine.
It's been a decade since New York's southern district last prosecuted the death penalty.
Accused Khalid Barnes has been convicted of murdering two drug suppliers but was eventually sentenced to life in prison in September 2009.
The Death Penitentiary was last carried out in New York, in 1953, when married couple Julius and Ethel Rosenberg were executed after they were convicted of spying for the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years earlier.
Both Rosenbergs were executed on June 19, 1953 on an electric mall.
According to judicial documents, Saipov, a native of Uzbekistan, showed a lack of regression in the days and months after the attack.
He told investigators he was feeling good about what he had done, police said.
Saipov told the authorities that after viewing a video of ISIS on his phone he was inspired to carry out an attack, according to the indictment.
He also demanded that he clinch the ISIS flag in his hospital room, police said.
He pleaded not guilty on 22 counts.
David Patton, one of the federal public defenders representing Saipov, said they were “obviously disillusioned” by a prosecution decision.
"We believe that the decision to seek the death penalty, and not to accept the plea of guilt for life in the prison without the possibility of relieving, will only prolong the trauma of these events for everyone involved," Patton said.
Saipov's defense team had previously asked the accuser not to seek the death penalty.
