Mr. Park has said the keystroughs factors of tighter controls at the North Korea-China border crossing and higher levies charged by people smugglers.
Pyongyang didn't comment publicly.
The vast majority of defectors from the north eventually received South Korean citizenship.
Seoul says that more than 30 000 North Koreans have illegally crossed the border since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
The majority escape through China, which has the longest border with North Korea and it is easier to cross than a heavily protected demilitarized zone (DMZ) between the two Koreas.
China considers defectors to illegal migrants, not refugees, and often violently repatriate them.
relations between the North and the South – which are still technical in the war – have significantly improved in recent months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of the two countries met in Pyongyang for talks aimed at pausing denuclearization negotiations.
This followed a historic meeting in June between U.S. President Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un in Singapore, when in a broad sense they agreed they were working to liberate the Korei Peninsula from nuclear weapons.
However, on Saturday Ri Yong-ho, North Korea's foreign minister, blamed U.S. sanctions that they had not made any progress ever since.
"Without any trust in the United States, there will be no confidence in our national security, and there is no question that under such circumstances we are the first to disarise ourselves," Mr. Ri said in a Speechibate at the U.N. General Assembly in New York.
Nancy Pelosi calls Brett Kavanaugh "hysterical," says he's incapacitated to serve at Supreme Court
Opposition leader Nancy Pelosi called Supreme Court Supreme Justice candidate Brett Kavanaugh “hysterical” and said she was incapacitated for service at the Supreme Court because of her temperament.
Pelosi commented in Saturday's interview at the Texas Tribune Festival in Austin, Texas.
“I couldn’t help but think I’d call a woman ‘historically like that,’” Pelosi described her reaction to Kavanaugh's testimony before the Senate Judiciary on Thursday.
Kavanaugh emotionally denied claims that he sexually abused Dr. Christine Blaysey Ford when they were both teenagers.
During the introductory statement, Kavanaugh was very emotional, occasionally nearly shouting and suffocating while discussing his family and high school years.
He also explicitly denounced Democracies on the committee, calling accusations against him "grotesque and coordinated denigrations" organized by the Liberals angry that Hillary Clinton lost the 2016 presidential election.
Pelosi said she believed Kavanaugh's testimony had proved she couldn't take part in the work of the Supreme Court because he showed he had prejudgments against the Democrat.
"I think he disqualifies himself with such statements and the way he's attacking Ms. Clinton and the Democrat," she said.
When asked if he will try to impeach Kavanaugh if it is confirmed, and if democracies get a majority in the House of Representatives, Pelosi stepped in.
“I’m going to say the following—If it didn’t say the truth to Congress or the FBI, then it's not capable not only of being in the Supreme Court, but it's in the court where it's right now," Pelosi said.
Kavanaugh is currently a judge of the Federal Court of Appeal in the District of Columbia.
Pelosi added that as a Democrat, she is worried about potential Kavanaugh's decisions against the Affordable Welfare Act or an Abortion Act stemming from Roe v. Wade proceedings, because she is regarded as a Conservative referee.
