How the U.S.-China trade war may be good for Beijing
Beginning of the U.S.-China trade war resonated loudly and, while the battle is far from the end, strife between countries may benefit Beijing in the long-running period, experts say.
Donald Trump, the U.S. president, fired the first warning earlier this year, taxing key Chinese exports, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The most notable escalation in this week has surfaced with new tariffs that have influencted $200bn (£150bn), actually taxing half of all commodities coming to the U.S. from China.
Pediatrics have reciprocated in some way each time, and a five-to-tnu ten per cent tariff at $60bn of U.S. merchandise has recently been imposed.
"China has vowed to address the U.S. for a blow, and the world's second-largest economy will certainly not be handed over so soon.
Withdrawal of Washington could be achieved by easing demands, but public subjudgation to the U.S. would be too embarrassing for Xi Jinping, China's president.
Nevertheless, experts say that if Beijing plays its cards well, pressure from the U.S. trade war can provide positive support in the long-term support for China by reducing the interdependence of the two economies.
"The fact that a rapid political decision in Washington or Beijing could create the conditions to trigger economic steadfast in both countries is actually much more dangerous than observers believed," said Abigail Grace, a research fellow for strategic deliberation focusing on Asia at the Center for New U.S. Security.
Syrmia 'ready' to resurrect refugees, foreign minister says
“Syria says it is ready for the voluntary retrieval of refugees and is appealing for assistance in rebuilding a country that has devastated more than seven years.
Addressing the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem said conditions in the country are improving.
"Today the situation on the ground is more stable and safer thanks to the progress made in the fight against terrorism," he said.
The government is proceeding to rehabilitatate areas devastated by terrorist attacks in order to establish a normal situation.
All the conditions for the voluntary return of refugees to a country that they had to leave because of terrorism and one-sided economic measures that focused on their daily lives and livelihoods have now been met.
The U.N. estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled the country since the start of the war in 2011.
The six-million-strong people who are still living in the country in need of humanitarian aid.
Al-Moualem said the Syrian regime would benefit from helping to renovate the devastated country.
However, he stressed that he would not accept conditional assistance or assistance from countries sponsoring the rebellion.
