New Red Tissue: Concentrations Decrease in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
The latest report by the Floride (FWC) Commission for the Preservation of Fish and Plant and Animal (FWC) shows a general decrease in red tide in parts of Tampa Bay.
According to FWC, heterogeneous algae flowering conditions are reported in areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier – which indicates a decrease in concentrations.
The red tide spans about 130 miles along the coast from the northern part of Pinellas County to Lee County.
Boutising areas can be found about 10 miles off the coast of Hillsborough County, but in smaller places compared to last week.
The red tide was also spotted in Pasco County.
In the past week there have been intermediate concentrations in Pinellas County or in the associated offshore region, low to high concentrations in the Collaborate District of Hillsborough, common to a high concentration in Manatee district, common to high concentrations in Sarasota County or the associated communal area, common up to a medium concentration in Charlotte County, common to a high-concentration in Lee district or a corresponding communiquency area, and a low-concentration.
Respiratory irrigated cases continue to be reported in pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
Respiratory Irritation has not been reported in northwestern Florida over the past week.
